1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,383
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,433 --> 00:00:02,816
Key Terms: Communicating Uncertainty.

3
00:00:02,866 --> 00:00:07,472
Thirty-four terms for matching the
strength of a claim to the evidence behind

4
00:00:07,522 --> 00:00:09,622
it, in words and in numbers.

5
00:00:09,672 --> 00:00:13,920
They let you write an honest report and
check an overconfident one.

6
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:15,291
Hallo.

7
00:00:15,341 --> 00:00:16,914
This is Liam, in for Bear.

8
00:00:16,964 --> 00:00:20,701
It is tempting to think a claim should
sound as strong as possible, with

9
00:00:20,751 --> 00:00:22,053
confident verbs and confident numbers.

10
00:00:22,103 --> 00:00:24,488
It should sound only as strong as its
evidence.

11
00:00:24,538 --> 00:00:28,816
This film goes through the terms for doing
that, each one shown as it is named,

12
00:00:28,866 --> 00:00:30,710
because the exam expects the exact term.

13
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:34,146
Four labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

14
00:00:34,196 --> 00:00:37,567
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

15
00:00:37,617 --> 00:00:40,703
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

16
00:00:40,753 --> 00:00:45,264
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

17
00:00:45,314 --> 00:00:48,400
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

18
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:50,198
This table is the map.

19
00:00:50,248 --> 00:00:54,092
Thirty-four terms in four groups, all
about matching what is claimed to the

20
00:00:54,142 --> 00:00:54,392
evidence.

21
00:00:54,442 --> 00:00:56,489
The first group is the verb ladder.

22
00:00:56,539 --> 00:01:00,384
It holds warranted assertion, the verb
taxonomy and the verb downgrade, and it

23
00:01:00,434 --> 00:01:03,080
matches the verb of a claim to its
evidence.

24
00:01:03,430 --> 00:01:05,827
The second group is writing it down.

25
00:01:05,877 --> 00:01:10,720
One document in layers, from the Layer one
summary to the Layer three appendix.

26
00:01:10,770 --> 00:01:14,564
Then naming what you do not know, with
epistemic uncertainty, aleatoric

27
00:01:14,614 --> 00:01:17,710
uncertainty, the throat-clearing hedge and
the peer critique protocol.

28
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:23,226
The third group is scoring calibration,
the numbers behind a stated probability.

29
00:01:23,276 --> 00:01:26,269
Calibration, the reliability diagram,
overconfidence and underconfidence.

30
00:01:26,319 --> 00:01:31,485
Then the Brier score, the proper scoring
rule, the Brier decomposition and

31
00:01:31,535 --> 00:01:31,920
resolution.

32
00:01:32,270 --> 00:01:34,864
The fourth group is where scores fail.

33
00:01:34,914 --> 00:01:36,752
Blind spots, fixes and guarantees.

34
00:01:36,802 --> 00:01:41,662
It runs from ECE, expected calibration
error, and its limits, through the Epic

35
00:01:41,712 --> 00:01:45,817
Sepsis Model and the evidence ladder, to
temperature scaling, distribution shift

36
00:01:45,867 --> 00:01:46,950
and conformal prediction.

37
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:51,606
A ladder of claim verbs, strongest at the
top, beside one result written with three

38
00:01:51,656 --> 00:01:51,897
verbs.

39
00:01:51,947 --> 00:01:55,672
Warranted assertion means your claim may
be only as strong as your evidence.

40
00:01:55,722 --> 00:01:57,995
Eighty-seven percent on one held-out set
supports observe.

41
00:01:58,045 --> 00:01:59,448
Find and conclude claim more.

42
00:01:59,498 --> 00:02:03,513
For example, a job seeker rewrites their
résumé and then gets an interview call.

43
00:02:03,563 --> 00:02:07,579
The evidence warrants saying the call came
after the rewrite, not that the rewrite

44
00:02:07,629 --> 00:02:08,160
caused it.

45
00:02:08,510 --> 00:02:11,401
The verb taxonomy is that ladder with its
order frozen.

46
00:02:11,451 --> 00:02:15,811
Eight claim verbs run from hypothesize at
the bottom to prove at the top, each

47
00:02:15,861 --> 00:02:17,282
beside the evidence it needs.

48
00:02:17,332 --> 00:02:19,634
A ladder that contradicts itself gives
false confidence.

49
00:02:19,684 --> 00:02:23,751
For example, two reviewers judge reports
on an agent that scores job listings for

50
00:02:23,801 --> 00:02:24,045
fit.

51
00:02:24,095 --> 00:02:28,456
With one fixed order, both treat suggest
as weaker than show, so the same evidence

52
00:02:28,506 --> 00:02:30,220
gets the same verb from either.

53
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:32,305
Observe is marked on the ladder.

54
00:02:32,355 --> 00:02:35,576
It reports what you measured, under stated
conditions, and stops there.

55
00:02:35,626 --> 00:02:40,038
The example observes an ECE of zero point
zero four on the global test set.

56
00:02:40,088 --> 00:02:42,417
For one experiment, observe is the safest
verb.

57
00:02:42,467 --> 00:02:46,581
For example, an agent's fit scores agree
with a recruiter's picks on this week's

58
00:02:46,631 --> 00:02:47,473
batch of listings.

59
00:02:47,523 --> 00:02:51,340
The honest sentence observes agreement on
that batch, and claims nothing beyond it.

60
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:53,239
Find sits one rung higher.

61
00:02:53,289 --> 00:02:57,717
Find says the result held up across
everything you examined, so it needs a

62
00:02:57,767 --> 00:02:59,636
comparison, a sample size and replication.

63
00:02:59,686 --> 00:03:01,556
Observe covers one set of conditions.

64
00:03:01,606 --> 00:03:04,434
Using find for a one-shot anecdote is the
misuse.

65
00:03:04,484 --> 00:03:07,633
For example, a cover-letter tool beats
hand-written letters on interview

66
00:03:07,683 --> 00:03:11,791
callbacks, with the number of applications
stated, and the advantage appears again in

67
00:03:11,841 --> 00:03:13,390
a second round of applications.

68
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:14,350
That earns find.

69
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:16,535
A checklist of what conclude needs.

70
00:03:16,585 --> 00:03:19,361
Conclude says the question behind the work
is closed.

71
00:03:19,411 --> 00:03:22,502
That takes full analysis, alternatives
named and rejected, sensitivity analysis,

72
00:03:22,552 --> 00:03:24,387
and a finding that survives it.

73
00:03:24,437 --> 00:03:26,586
Suggest only says the data are consistent.

74
00:03:26,636 --> 00:03:27,842
Few claims warrant it.

75
00:03:27,892 --> 00:03:31,611
For example, to conclude that a résumé-
tailoring agent raises interview rates,

76
00:03:31,661 --> 00:03:36,637
you must rule out rivals, such as a busier
hiring season, and check that the result

77
00:03:36,687 --> 00:03:37,579
survives sensitivity analysis.

78
00:03:37,629 --> 00:03:40,720
Short of that, use a weaker verb, such as
suggest.

79
00:03:41,070 --> 00:03:43,660
An AI-written sentence claims it
conclusively demonstrates a result.

80
00:03:43,710 --> 00:03:45,419
The fluency-trap detector is a habit.

81
00:03:45,469 --> 00:03:49,525
When AI text uses a strong verb, stop and
ask where the evidence is.

82
00:03:49,575 --> 00:03:53,338
The red crosses show none of the five
kinds is in the paragraph.

83
00:03:53,388 --> 00:03:56,858
For example, an AI job assistant writes
that its tailoring clearly demonstrates

84
00:03:56,908 --> 00:03:59,204
you are a top match for the role.

85
00:03:59,254 --> 00:04:00,377
Demonstrates is the flag.

86
00:04:00,427 --> 00:04:03,310
Ask what evidence stands behind it before
you send anything.

87
00:04:03,660 --> 00:04:06,963
The red marker moves down the ladder, from
conclusively demonstrates to suggests.

88
00:04:07,013 --> 00:04:08,360
That is a verb downgrade.

89
00:04:08,410 --> 00:04:11,713
You replace the verb with a weaker one the
evidence can carry.

90
00:04:11,763 --> 00:04:14,508
The facts are unchanged, but the decision
may not be.

91
00:04:14,558 --> 00:04:18,699
For example, an agent's summary concludes
that a listing is the right job for you.

92
00:04:18,749 --> 00:04:22,611
The evidence is a keyword match, so the
downgrade says the keyword match suggests

93
00:04:22,661 --> 00:04:23,170
a fit.

94
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:24,938
Two boxes share the verb ladder.

95
00:04:24,988 --> 00:04:26,160
This is build and audit.

96
00:04:26,210 --> 00:04:29,340
Build is your own report, where you match
each verb to its proof.

97
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:33,009
Audit is an AI claim handed to you, where
you lower the verbs that overreach.

98
00:04:33,059 --> 00:04:34,232
One supervisory skill, not two.

99
00:04:34,282 --> 00:04:37,901
For example, you build when you write up
your own job-search agent and tie each

100
00:04:37,951 --> 00:04:38,879
verb to a test.

101
00:04:38,929 --> 00:04:43,037
You audit when an AI summary says a tool
is proven to win more interviews, and you

102
00:04:43,087 --> 00:04:44,260
lower that verb to fit.

103
00:04:44,610 --> 00:04:47,088
One page in three layers, and who reads
which.

104
00:04:47,138 --> 00:04:49,055
The Layer one summary starts every
section.

105
00:04:49,105 --> 00:04:52,987
In plain words, it gives the finding and
what it means for the decision.

106
00:04:53,037 --> 00:04:56,639
In the case shown, about eighty-nine in
one hundred patients with the condition

107
00:04:56,689 --> 00:04:57,201
are flagged.

108
00:04:57,251 --> 00:05:01,415
For example, a report on a listing-scoring
agent opens with a plain sentence for the

109
00:05:01,465 --> 00:05:01,976
job seeker.

110
00:05:02,026 --> 00:05:05,909
Most listings it ranked highest were ones
a career adviser also rated worth applying

111
00:05:05,959 --> 00:05:06,190
to.

112
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:10,575
Layer two technical detail is for the
reader who has to judge the work.

113
00:05:10,625 --> 00:05:12,618
It carries the methods, equations and
tables.

114
00:05:12,668 --> 00:05:16,703
The same case becomes a sensitivity of
zero point eight nine, and zero point

115
00:05:16,753 --> 00:05:18,454
eight one for patients over seventy-five.

116
00:05:18,504 --> 00:05:22,539
For example, Layer two of the listing
report gives the scoring method, how many

117
00:05:22,589 --> 00:05:26,625
listings were checked, the agreement rate
with its interval, and the job types where

118
00:05:26,675 --> 00:05:27,500
agreement was weaker.

119
00:05:27,850 --> 00:05:31,870
The Layer three appendix is what another
team needs to rerun the work.

120
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:34,374
Code, settings, data documentation and
every sensitivity analysis.

121
00:05:34,424 --> 00:05:36,252
Only the auditor goes that deep.

122
00:05:36,302 --> 00:05:40,009
All three layers stay in one document,
because two documents drift apart.

123
00:05:40,059 --> 00:05:43,765
For example, the listing report's appendix
holds the agent's prompts, its model

124
00:05:43,815 --> 00:05:48,148
settings, and the postings it scored with
how they were collected, so another team

125
00:05:48,198 --> 00:05:49,400
could score them again.

126
00:05:49,750 --> 00:05:53,855
Four red bars show the interval around an
accuracy of zero point eight seven, in

127
00:05:53,905 --> 00:05:54,686
this made-up example.

128
00:05:54,736 --> 00:05:57,733
Epistemic uncertainty comes from not
knowing enough yet, so more evidence

129
00:05:57,783 --> 00:05:58,287
shrinks it.

130
00:05:58,337 --> 00:06:01,335
From fifty cases to three thousand two
hundred, the bar narrows.

131
00:06:01,385 --> 00:06:05,490
For example, a model that predicts who
gets an interview has been checked on only

132
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:06,875
a small batch of applications.

133
00:06:06,925 --> 00:06:11,030
Much of its uncertainty is epistemic, and
checking it on many more would narrow it.

134
00:06:11,380 --> 00:06:13,460
Now the bars stay the same length.

135
00:06:13,510 --> 00:06:17,719
Aleatoric uncertainty is noise built into
the system, and no amount of extra data

136
00:06:17,769 --> 00:06:18,328
removes it.

137
00:06:18,378 --> 00:06:22,587
In these simulated samples, the best
possible score stays near the red floor at

138
00:06:22,637 --> 00:06:23,804
zero point two one.

139
00:06:23,854 --> 00:06:28,064
For example, two equally strong candidates
apply for the same role, and the outcome

140
00:06:28,114 --> 00:06:31,106
turns on which recruiter happens to open
which file first.

141
00:06:31,156 --> 00:06:33,540
No extra data about the candidates removes
that.

142
00:06:33,890 --> 00:06:35,385
A sentence is struck out.

143
00:06:35,435 --> 00:06:37,548
It only says further work is needed.

144
00:06:37,598 --> 00:06:39,092
That is a throat-clearing hedge.

145
00:06:39,142 --> 00:06:41,873
It sounds careful but points at no
particular gap.

146
00:06:41,923 --> 00:06:44,963
The replacement names the uncertainty,
says epistemic or aleatoric, quantifies

147
00:06:45,013 --> 00:06:47,126
it, and says what would change it.

148
00:06:47,176 --> 00:06:50,524
For example, a salary-estimate tool's
report ends with, results may vary.

149
00:06:50,574 --> 00:06:55,159
A real replacement gives the range of the
estimates for remote roles, the widest of

150
00:06:55,209 --> 00:06:59,484
any, an epistemic gap because few such
postings were in the data, and more

151
00:06:59,534 --> 00:07:00,720
postings would narrow it.

152
00:07:01,070 --> 00:07:02,903
Six steps, each with its dots.

153
00:07:02,953 --> 00:07:05,101
The peer critique protocol makes review
specific.

154
00:07:05,151 --> 00:07:08,553
Three things that work, three verb
mismatches, three evasive hedges, one

155
00:07:08,603 --> 00:07:10,437
structural concern and one open question.

156
00:07:10,487 --> 00:07:12,634
Peers see the mismatches the writer
cannot.

157
00:07:12,684 --> 00:07:16,714
For example, a classmate reviewing your
write-up on a cover-letter agent does not

158
00:07:16,764 --> 00:07:18,598
just say it sounds too confident.

159
00:07:18,648 --> 00:07:22,678
They quote your sentence that the agent
improves replies, and note the evidence

160
00:07:22,728 --> 00:07:23,620
only supports observe.

161
00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:28,500
A grid of one hundred dots, one for each
case where the model says eighty percent.

162
00:07:28,550 --> 00:07:31,076
In this simulated run, eighty-three are
filled, meaning right.

163
00:07:31,126 --> 00:07:34,511
Calibration means the stated chance
matches how often the model is right.

164
00:07:34,561 --> 00:07:35,656
It is not accuracy.

165
00:07:35,706 --> 00:07:39,377
For example, an interview predictor gives
a set of applications an eighty percent

166
00:07:39,427 --> 00:07:39,663
chance.

167
00:07:39,713 --> 00:07:43,670
If it is calibrated, about eighty of every
hundred of them really get interviews.

168
00:07:44,020 --> 00:07:47,230
This plot is a reliability diagram, also
called a calibration curve.

169
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:50,787
Stated confidence runs along the bottom
and actual accuracy up the side.

170
00:07:50,837 --> 00:07:53,751
Each of the ten points is one bin of
predictions.

171
00:07:53,801 --> 00:07:56,419
A perfectly calibrated model sits on the
dashed diagonal.

172
00:07:56,469 --> 00:08:00,568
For example, a tool gives each job listing
a chance that it is fake.

173
00:08:00,618 --> 00:08:04,718
Its reliability diagram groups listings by
that chance and plots the share in each

174
00:08:04,768 --> 00:08:06,200
group that really were fake.

175
00:08:06,550 --> 00:08:08,760
The red curve falls below the diagonal.

176
00:08:08,810 --> 00:08:09,729
That is overconfidence.

177
00:08:09,779 --> 00:08:12,636
The model claims more certainty than its
results earn.

178
00:08:12,686 --> 00:08:17,156
In this simulated bin it says eighty-five
percent and is right sixty-two percent of

179
00:08:17,206 --> 00:08:17,802
the time.

180
00:08:17,852 --> 00:08:19,740
Common in deep networks, not universal.

181
00:08:19,790 --> 00:08:23,615
For example, a résumé checker tells job
seekers their applications will very

182
00:08:23,665 --> 00:08:27,490
likely pass the applicant-tracking screen,
but far fewer pass than it said.

183
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:30,371
Here the red curve rises above the
diagonal.

184
00:08:30,421 --> 00:08:31,340
That is underconfidence.

185
00:08:31,390 --> 00:08:34,244
The model claims less certainty than its
results earn.

186
00:08:34,294 --> 00:08:38,116
It says seventy-five percent and is right
eighty percent of the time.

187
00:08:38,166 --> 00:08:41,020
Below the diagonal is overconfidence,
above it is underconfidence.

188
00:08:41,070 --> 00:08:45,860
For example, an agent tells a job seeker
that recruiters will likely reply to their

189
00:08:45,910 --> 00:08:49,410
messages, yet replies come even more often
than its stated chance.

190
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:51,115
Five bars, one per prediction.

191
00:08:51,165 --> 00:08:54,766
The height is the squared distance between
the stated probability and what happened,

192
00:08:54,816 --> 00:08:55,609
zero or one.

193
00:08:55,659 --> 00:08:59,823
The Brier score is their average, here
zero point one five eight, the red line.

194
00:08:59,873 --> 00:09:02,070
The tall red bar is a confident miss.

195
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:06,845
For example, a tool says a listing is very
likely still open, and the job turns out

196
00:09:06,895 --> 00:09:07,688
to be filled.

197
00:09:07,738 --> 00:09:11,340
That confident miss adds a large squared
gap to the tool's Brier score.

198
00:09:11,690 --> 00:09:15,771
The curve is the expected Brier score for
each probability the model could report,

199
00:09:15,821 --> 00:09:18,131
when the true chance is zero point seven.

200
00:09:18,181 --> 00:09:20,787
The lowest point, the red dot, sits
exactly there.

201
00:09:20,837 --> 00:09:22,852
That makes it a proper scoring rule.

202
00:09:22,902 --> 00:09:24,033
Honest reporting scores best.

203
00:09:24,083 --> 00:09:28,164
For example, an agent that forecasts each
job seeker's chance of an offer could

204
00:09:28,214 --> 00:09:29,934
inflate every number to sound decisive.

205
00:09:29,984 --> 00:09:33,770
Under a proper scoring rule, that
inflation scores worse on average than its

206
00:09:33,820 --> 00:09:34,360
honest estimate.

207
00:09:34,710 --> 00:09:37,082
Four bars split the score into parts.

208
00:09:37,132 --> 00:09:40,543
The Brier decomposition is uncertainty,
minus resolution, plus calibration error.

209
00:09:40,593 --> 00:09:46,080
Here, zero point one six, minus zero point
zero four five, plus zero point zero two

210
00:09:46,130 --> 00:09:48,502
eight, gives zero point one four three.

211
00:09:48,552 --> 00:09:52,309
For example, two tools that predict
recruiter replies earn similar Brier

212
00:09:52,359 --> 00:09:52,655
scores.

213
00:09:52,705 --> 00:09:56,807
Splitting them shows that one tells
messages apart but states the wrong

214
00:09:56,857 --> 00:10:00,960
chances, while the other states honest
chances but barely tells messages apart.

215
00:10:01,310 --> 00:10:04,396
Resolution, also called discrimination,
asks whether the model gives different

216
00:10:04,446 --> 00:10:07,218
cases different numbers, instead of
hugging the base rate.

217
00:10:07,268 --> 00:10:09,726
The grey bar always guesses zero point
two.

218
00:10:09,776 --> 00:10:10,980
It has zero resolution.

219
00:10:11,030 --> 00:10:14,116
The model beats it by zero point zero one
seven.

220
00:10:14,166 --> 00:10:17,565
For example, an interview predictor that
gives every applicant the overall

221
00:10:17,615 --> 00:10:19,133
interview rate has no resolution.

222
00:10:19,183 --> 00:10:24,150
One that gives strong fits a higher chance
and weak fits a lower one has resolution.

223
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,638
Bars of bin gaps from two hundred fifty
simulated predictions.

224
00:10:27,688 --> 00:10:30,826
ECE, expected calibration error, groups
predictions into bins by confidence.

225
00:10:30,876 --> 00:10:34,970
In each bin it compares confidence with
accuracy, then averages the gaps, with

226
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:36,245
fuller bins counting more.

227
00:10:36,295 --> 00:10:38,158
Here, zero point one zero six.

228
00:10:38,208 --> 00:10:42,621
For example, the ECE of a tool that
predicts which résumés pass a screen

229
00:10:42,671 --> 00:10:47,084
compares, in each confidence bin, the
chance it stated with the share of résumés

230
00:10:47,134 --> 00:10:48,040
that really passed.

231
00:10:48,390 --> 00:10:50,280
The same predictions with four bin counts.

232
00:10:50,330 --> 00:10:53,050
At five bins the ECE is zero point zero
nine.

233
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:55,821
At twenty bins it is zero point one one
six.

234
00:10:55,871 --> 00:10:59,146
That is bin dependence, so the bin count
must always be reported.

235
00:10:59,196 --> 00:11:03,302
For example, a vendor advertises a low ECE
for its résumé screener without the bin

236
00:11:03,352 --> 00:11:03,580
count.

237
00:11:03,630 --> 00:11:07,459
With a different bin count the number
could come out different, so nobody can

238
00:11:07,509 --> 00:11:08,290
check the claim.

239
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:10,168
The top bar turns red.

240
00:11:10,218 --> 00:11:13,008
MCE, maximum calibration error, reports
only the worst bin.

241
00:11:13,058 --> 00:11:16,795
Here MCE is zero point two zero seven,
about twice the ECE.

242
00:11:16,845 --> 00:11:20,582
A low ECE can hide one confidence range
that is badly off.

243
00:11:20,632 --> 00:11:24,053
For example, a recruiter's tool that
predicts whether candidates accept offers

244
00:11:24,103 --> 00:11:28,155
looks fine on average, but in one
confidence range its stated chances are

245
00:11:28,205 --> 00:11:28,787
far off.

246
00:11:28,837 --> 00:11:30,680
MCE points straight at that range.

247
00:11:31,030 --> 00:11:36,203
The top bar is the global ECE, with five
patient groups below, from an example

248
00:11:36,253 --> 00:11:36,551
table.

249
00:11:36,601 --> 00:11:41,078
Subgroup ECE repeats the calculation
inside each named group and sets it beside

250
00:11:41,128 --> 00:11:42,123
the global value.

251
00:11:42,173 --> 00:11:46,301
Rare-disease cases sit far above it, at
zero point one zero three.

252
00:11:46,351 --> 00:11:50,479
For example, a résumé screener looks
calibrated across all applicants, but its

253
00:11:50,529 --> 00:11:53,265
stated chances are far off for career
changers.

254
00:11:53,315 --> 00:11:56,747
Subgroup ECE reports career changers,
recent graduates and returners separately,

255
00:11:56,797 --> 00:11:58,140
beside the global value.

256
00:11:58,490 --> 00:12:03,067
A dashed line marks twice the global ECE,
and each group shows its ratio.

257
00:12:03,117 --> 00:12:05,711
Three of the five groups cross the line.

258
00:12:05,761 --> 00:12:08,355
Rare-disease cases reach five point seven
times global.

259
00:12:08,405 --> 00:12:11,990
Whether twice global should halt a
deployment is a values question.

260
00:12:12,340 --> 00:12:16,939
The Epic Sepsis Model is a sepsis warning
tool used in hundreds of hospitals.

261
00:12:16,989 --> 00:12:20,925
External validation means testing a model
on a new site's own patients.

262
00:12:20,975 --> 00:12:25,242
At one outside hospital, its ranking
score, in red, fell below the developer's

263
00:12:25,292 --> 00:12:25,574
range.

264
00:12:25,624 --> 00:12:29,559
It missed sixty-seven percent of sepsis
cases, and eighty-eight percent of alerts

265
00:12:29,609 --> 00:12:30,555
were false alarms.

266
00:12:30,605 --> 00:12:34,873
For example, an interview predictor built
on one university's graduates is tested on

267
00:12:34,923 --> 00:12:38,858
another university's graduates, with their
own records, before that school relies on

268
00:12:38,908 --> 00:12:39,190
it.

269
00:12:39,540 --> 00:12:43,502
Six rows, each pairing a level of evidence
with one verb.

270
00:12:43,552 --> 00:12:45,326
This is the evidence ladder.

271
00:12:45,376 --> 00:12:47,879
An ECE measured only internally licenses
hypothesize.

272
00:12:47,929 --> 00:12:49,703
A held-out test licenses observe.

273
00:12:49,753 --> 00:12:51,162
Subgroup ECE licenses find.

274
00:12:51,212 --> 00:12:53,350
Only validation in deployment licenses
conclude.

275
00:12:53,400 --> 00:12:55,538
Trace each claim to one row.

276
00:12:55,588 --> 00:13:00,280
For example, a job-matching agent checked
only on its own tuning data earns

277
00:13:00,330 --> 00:13:00,645
hypothesize.

278
00:13:00,695 --> 00:13:06,116
Only after it holds up for real job
seekers in their actual searches can its

279
00:13:06,166 --> 00:13:07,210
makers write conclude.

280
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:09,815
Three bars are the model's simulated
probabilities.

281
00:13:09,865 --> 00:13:13,437
Temperature scaling divides the raw
scores, called logits, by one fitted

282
00:13:13,487 --> 00:13:14,096
number, T.

283
00:13:14,146 --> 00:13:18,047
Confidence softens, from zero point seven
seven to zero point five nine.

284
00:13:18,097 --> 00:13:21,340
The top class stays on top, so accuracy is
untouched.

285
00:13:21,390 --> 00:13:25,621
For example, a listing-fit scorer gives
most listings chances that are too high.

286
00:13:25,671 --> 00:13:29,902
Temperature scaling softens them all with
one number, and every listing keeps the

287
00:13:29,952 --> 00:13:30,890
same top prediction.

288
00:13:31,240 --> 00:13:34,011
The curve is the loss at each value of T.

289
00:13:34,061 --> 00:13:38,242
The calibration set is data kept aside for
one job, fitting T, once the model's

290
00:13:38,292 --> 00:13:39,089
weights are frozen.

291
00:13:39,139 --> 00:13:43,038
Here the loss is lowest at the red dot,
near one point seven five.

292
00:13:43,088 --> 00:13:46,987
For example, a team holds back a batch of
past applications its interview predictor

293
00:13:47,037 --> 00:13:50,090
never trained on, and uses that batch only
to fit T.

294
00:13:50,440 --> 00:13:51,288
Three curves now.

295
00:13:51,338 --> 00:13:52,485
Grey is before scaling.

296
00:13:52,535 --> 00:13:55,777
Black is after scaling at the same
hospital, on the diagonal.

297
00:13:55,827 --> 00:13:59,369
Red is the same fix at a new hospital, off
it again.

298
00:13:59,419 --> 00:14:00,566
That is distribution shift.

299
00:14:00,616 --> 00:14:02,361
Deployment patients differ from
calibration patients.

300
00:14:02,411 --> 00:14:05,953
For example, an interview predictor
calibrated during a busy hiring season is

301
00:14:06,003 --> 00:14:07,150
used during a slowdown.

302
00:14:07,200 --> 00:14:11,340
The applicants and the odds have changed,
so its corrected chances no longer hold.

303
00:14:11,690 --> 00:14:14,281
Conformal prediction returns a set of
labels, not one.

304
00:14:14,331 --> 00:14:17,216
A cutoff on calibration scores decides
which labels get in.

305
00:14:17,266 --> 00:14:21,617
If the data are exchangeable, the set
covers the truth at least at the target

306
00:14:21,667 --> 00:14:23,378
rate, as the black bar shows.

307
00:14:23,428 --> 00:14:25,726
After a shift, the red bar falls short.

308
00:14:25,776 --> 00:14:30,128
For example, a salary tool returns every
pay band that passes the cutoff, not one

309
00:14:30,178 --> 00:14:30,421
guess.

310
00:14:30,471 --> 00:14:35,116
That set holds the actual offer at least
at the target rate, if new offers are

311
00:14:35,166 --> 00:14:36,290
exchangeable with past ones.

312
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:40,951
Two rows of dots, and both models are
right nine times out of ten.

313
00:14:41,001 --> 00:14:42,197
They are equally accurate.

314
00:14:42,247 --> 00:14:44,689
Accurate means how often the model is
right.

315
00:14:44,739 --> 00:14:46,870
Calibrated means the stated chance matches
that.

316
00:14:46,920 --> 00:14:49,985
The left model says ninety-nine percent,
so it is overconfident.

317
00:14:50,035 --> 00:14:53,100
The right model says ninety percent, and
it is calibrated.

318
00:14:53,450 --> 00:14:58,212
Left, the bars shrink as the number of
cases grows from fifty to three thousand

319
00:14:58,262 --> 00:14:58,854
two hundred.

320
00:14:58,904 --> 00:15:02,062
That is epistemic uncertainty, and
collecting more cases reduces it.

321
00:15:02,112 --> 00:15:04,307
Right, the bars stay the same height.

322
00:15:04,357 --> 00:15:08,798
That is aleatoric uncertainty, noise in
the system itself, and no amount of data

323
00:15:08,848 --> 00:15:09,440
moves it.

324
00:15:09,790 --> 00:15:12,488
The same bin gaps are drawn on both sides.

325
00:15:12,538 --> 00:15:17,069
Left, the black line is ECE, the size-
weighted average gap, zero point one zero

326
00:15:17,119 --> 00:15:18,596
six in the simulated run.

327
00:15:18,646 --> 00:15:23,176
Right, the red bar is MCE, the one biggest
gap, zero point two zero seven.

328
00:15:23,226 --> 00:15:26,230
Report both, because the average can hide
the worst bin.

329
00:15:26,580 --> 00:15:30,390
Both sides use a number called T, and that
is the trap.

330
00:15:30,440 --> 00:15:34,250
Temperature scaling chooses T from held-
out data so stated probabilities match

331
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:36,823
results, and the top class stays on top.

332
00:15:36,873 --> 00:15:41,648
Sampling temperature is a dial the user
turns to make generated text more or less

333
00:15:41,698 --> 00:15:41,970
varied.

334
00:15:42,020 --> 00:15:43,900
One calibrates, the other adds variety.

335
00:15:44,250 --> 00:15:46,802
Left, a vague claim with its exact
version.

336
00:15:46,852 --> 00:15:48,428
Right, what it rests on.

337
00:15:48,478 --> 00:15:51,680
The vague claim concludes the new model is
more accurate.

338
00:15:51,730 --> 00:15:54,932
The exact one observes eighty-seven
percent on one held-out set.

339
00:15:54,982 --> 00:15:58,510
In this made-up example the intervals
overlap, so observe, not conclude.

340
00:15:58,860 --> 00:16:01,220
The vague claim says the model is
overconfident.

341
00:16:01,270 --> 00:16:06,340
The exact one says that at a stated ninety
percent it is right seventy percent of the

342
00:16:06,390 --> 00:16:06,642
time.

343
00:16:06,692 --> 00:16:08,449
Grey is stated, red is actual.

344
00:16:08,499 --> 00:16:11,160
The gap is zero point two below the
diagonal.

345
00:16:11,510 --> 00:16:14,368
The vague claim says the Brier score looks
fine.

346
00:16:14,418 --> 00:16:18,891
The exact one gives the score, zero point
one four three, and a baseline.

347
00:16:18,941 --> 00:16:22,122
Always saying zero point two scores zero
point one six.

348
00:16:22,172 --> 00:16:25,030
The model wins by zero point zero one
seven.

349
00:16:25,380 --> 00:16:27,970
The vague claim says the ECE is low.

350
00:16:28,020 --> 00:16:33,250
The exact one says the simulated ECE is
zero point one zero six at ten equal-width

351
00:16:33,300 --> 00:16:38,200
bins, zero point zero nine at five and
zero point one one six at twenty.

352
00:16:38,250 --> 00:16:39,520
That is bin dependence.

353
00:16:39,870 --> 00:16:42,944
The vague claim says performance is fine
for everyone.

354
00:16:42,994 --> 00:16:48,151
The exact one says rare-disease ECE is
zero point one zero three against a global

355
00:16:48,201 --> 00:16:51,970
zero point zero one eight, five point
seven times as high.

356
00:16:52,020 --> 00:16:54,400
The black line is the global value.

357
00:16:54,750 --> 00:16:57,859
A checklist of six questions for any claim
about uncertainty.

358
00:16:57,909 --> 00:16:59,438
The first is now ticked.

359
00:16:59,488 --> 00:17:01,965
Check the verb against the evidence behind
it.

360
00:17:02,015 --> 00:17:05,439
That is warranted assertion, with the verb
taxonomy as the ruler.

361
00:17:05,489 --> 00:17:07,650
Much writing sits one notch too high.

362
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,258
The second question is for AI-written
text.

363
00:17:10,308 --> 00:17:11,577
Run the fluency-trap detector.

364
00:17:11,627 --> 00:17:15,863
Every strong verb is a flag until you find
the evidence for it.

365
00:17:15,913 --> 00:17:18,830
Where the evidence is thin, apply a verb
downgrade.

366
00:17:19,180 --> 00:17:20,378
Third, the ECE number.

367
00:17:20,428 --> 00:17:24,124
Because of bin dependence, an ECE means
little without its bin count.

368
00:17:24,174 --> 00:17:29,118
And because an average can hide one bad
range, ask for MCE, the worst bin, beside

369
00:17:29,168 --> 00:17:29,430
it.

370
00:17:29,780 --> 00:17:30,621
Fourth, the groups.

371
00:17:30,671 --> 00:17:35,075
Ask for subgroup ECE, one value for each
named group, set beside the global ECE.

372
00:17:35,125 --> 00:17:40,123
A model can be calibrated on average and
still be off for the group in front of

373
00:17:40,173 --> 00:17:40,420
you.

374
00:17:40,770 --> 00:17:42,123
Fifth, the uncertainty itself.

375
00:17:42,173 --> 00:17:45,982
It should be named, as epistemic
uncertainty, which more evidence would

376
00:17:46,032 --> 00:17:50,543
shrink, or aleatoric uncertainty, which it
would not, with what would change the

377
00:17:50,593 --> 00:17:50,894
finding.

378
00:17:50,944 --> 00:17:53,350
A throat-clearing hedge does none of that.

379
00:17:53,700 --> 00:17:55,749
Sixth, where the fix was tested.

380
00:17:55,799 --> 00:17:59,246
Temperature scaling fitted on a
calibration set can fail under

381
00:17:59,296 --> 00:18:02,045
distribution shift, so ask about the
deployment population.

382
00:18:02,095 --> 00:18:03,444
Then say it exactly.

383
00:18:03,494 --> 00:18:09,040
Not conclude, but observe an ECE of zero
point zero four, ten bins, one test set.

384
00:18:09,890 --> 00:18:10,441
Let's recap.

385
00:18:10,491 --> 00:18:13,749
The verb of a claim must match the
evidence behind it.

386
00:18:13,799 --> 00:18:16,756
A verb downgrade keeps the facts and
lowers the posture.

387
00:18:16,806 --> 00:18:20,064
Name the uncertainty, epistemic or
aleatoric, and what would change it.

388
00:18:20,114 --> 00:18:23,673
Calibration means the stated chance
matches how often the model is right.

389
00:18:23,723 --> 00:18:27,582
Report ECE with its bin count, with MCE
and subgroup ECE beside it.

390
00:18:27,632 --> 00:18:30,890
A fix fitted on one population can fail
under distribution shift.

391
00:18:31,240 --> 00:18:32,867
Quiz me on communicating uncertainty.

392
00:18:32,917 --> 00:18:35,550
Give me one short case at a time.

393
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:40,917
For each case I must name the exact term
and say why its look-alike is wrong.

394
00:18:40,967 --> 00:18:44,271
Use these terms, observe, find, conclude,
verb downgrade, epistemic uncertainty,

395
00:18:44,321 --> 00:18:46,954
aleatoric uncertainty, calibration, ECE,
MCE and distribution shift.

396
00:18:47,004 --> 00:18:51,650
Do not show the answer until I reply, then
correct me in one sentence.

397
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,115
Key Terms: Communicating Uncertainty.

398
00:18:54,165 --> 00:18:56,280
At Nik Bear Brown.
